  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective implementation of the National Action Plan for the elimination of the worst forms of child labour, including in plantation sectors</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective implementation of the National Action Plan for the elimination of the worst forms of child labour, including in the plantation sector, is critical to ensuring children&apos;s rights are protected and preventing the exploitation of children in employment. It is important to involve all parties, including the government, private sector, civil society, and communities, as the issue is complex and multi-factorial. 
The National Action Plan on the Elimination of the Worst Forms of Child Labor (Continued until 2045) is a document that needs to be a reference for Indonesia&apos;s desire to create a generation that is resilient and able to move on in life. This document also serves as a guideline for national constituents to encourage the emergence of a new workforce that is safer, healthier and able to improve the quality of employment.
In line with the Indonesia DWCP 2020-2025, ILO has been working to strengthen the capacities of tripartite constituents and relevant stakeholders in the elimination of child labour including in palm oil and the fisheries industry. ILO support has included the development of strategies and policies that led to the preparation of the Roadmap 2024 &#x2013; 2029.
In 2024 - 2025 ILO Jakarta supports the formulation of an Advanced Child Labour-Free Indonesia Roadmap: (A Vision for a) Golden Indonesia Without Child Labour 2024 - 2045, as well as support activities to eliminate child labour, particularly in the Plantation sector. ILO works together with the government, employers&apos; unions, trade unions, media, universities, and non-governmental organizations in conducting activities, both in the form of direct support and technical assistance.
The CPO is essential in the fight against child labour and forced labour, while also promoting decent work. It encourages initiatives that foster better working conditions across supply chains in various sectors, particularly in the palm oil industry, as a strategy to prevent child labour and forced labour.</narrative>
  </description>
